@node Arrangements
@section Arrangements

An arrangement is an indexed representation of a collection, and often a
far more efficient one than a stream of update triples.  Many operators
do the same thing with their input: they build and maintain an index so
they can look updates up later.  An arrangement builds that index once
and shares it, so the cost of indexing, in communication, computation
and memory, is paid a single time across every operator that reads it.

@subsection Building an arrangement

@code{dtfl_collection_arrange} indexes a collection by its whole record;
@code{dtfl_collection_arrange_by_key} indexes a collection of
@code{key || val} records by key, which is the form join and reduce
read.  Each returns a @code{dtfl_arranged} handle:

@example
dtfl_arranged *knows = dtfl_collection_arrange_by_key(c, u64, u64,
    "knows");
@end example

@code{dtfl_arranged_as_collection} reads an arrangement back as a
collection; the arranged forms of join and reduce
(@code{dtfl_arranged_join_core}, @code{dtfl_arranged_reduce_core}) take
arrangements directly, so two operators sharing one index do not each
rebuild it.

@subsection Sharing across dataflows

An arrangement can be shared not only within a dataflow but
@emph{across} dataflows on the same worker.  @code{dtfl_arranged_trace}
extracts a shareable trace handle, a new reader of the shared index that
may outlive the arrangement.  @code{dtfl_trace_import} brings that trace
into another dataflow on the same worker, producing a second arrangement
over the same backing data.  This is how one maintained collection
serves an arbitrary number of later queries at almost no additional
cost: build and arrange it in one dataflow, keep the trace, and import
it wherever a query needs it.

Trace sharing is worker-local: a trace imports only into dataflows on
the worker that created it (@pxref{Threads}).  A trace handle holds the
shared index back from compacting past its frontier, so free it with
@code{dtfl_trace_free} when done; a handle never freed holds compaction
forever.

@subsection Trace wrappers and compaction

An arrangement maintains its updates in a compact form, merging batches
as they accumulate so every reader has an efficient index of all updates
so far.  As a program advances its inputs and no longer needs to
distinguish old times, the trace @emph{compacts}: it coarsens the times
of old updates (down to the frontier its readers still hold) and
combines updates that have become indistinguishable, which bounds the
memory an arrangement uses even as the stream of updates grows without
end.
